The MS-DOS side of hsdefrag.exe
===============================

hsdefrag.exe is a dual-mode program. In Windows it runs as a Windows program;
started in MS-DOS it is an MS-DOS program that defragments FAT12/16/32
volumes: in MS-DOS mode of Windows 95/98, from a startup disk, and in the
boot-time pass that "hsdefrag X: --boot-defrag" schedules on Windows 95/98.
The MS-DOS side is built from src/fatdos.c and the portable engine in src/
(see the Makefile).

The MS-DOS side is a 32-bit protected-mode program:

  - Built with DJGPP, the GNU C compiler and C library for MS-DOS.
    The latest DJGPP is available at https://www.delorie.com/djgpp/
    Its library is used unmodified; COPYING.DJ gives the terms
    (with COPYING and COPYING.LIB, which it refers to).

  - It runs on CWSDPMI, the free DPMI host by Charles W. Sandmann, which is
    bound into hsdefrag.exe as its starter stub (cwsdstub.exe here). The
    source code of CWSDPMI is available at
      https://www.delorie.com/pub/djgpp/current/v2misc/csdpmi7s.zip
    and the official binaries at
      https://www.delorie.com/pub/djgpp/current/v2misc/csdpmi7b.zip
    COPYING.CWS gives the terms (with COPYING, the GNU GPL version 2).

cwsdstub.exe is bin/CWSDSTUB.EXE from csdpmi7b.zip (release 7) with one of
its documented parameters changed, as CWSPARAM.EXE from the same package
does: the paging file is set to "" (none), so CWSDPMI never creates or
writes c:\cwsdpmi.swp. A swap file growing on the volume that is being
defragmented would change it underneath the defragmenter. The only
difference from the official file is the swap-file name in the CWSPBLK
parameter block (file offsets 21390-21403, 0x538E-0x539B, zero-filled):

  official   15cb50f3cc7022cd1148b4569813b33f4b56c3f31ebdd296c7f97906d2543625
  this file  765b86aa0fc287589adbeae9fd3b4acf9a15a151ae94d30f50cf1867154a92d4
  (SHA-256)

Like any DJGPP program, the MS-DOS side is the stub followed by the COFF
image of the program; on its own it would be

  cat dos/cwsdstub.exe build/dos/hsdefrag > hsdefrag-dos.exe

tools/mkdual.c puts both into hsdefrag.exe, after the PE headers of the
Windows program (build/hsdefrag-win.exe) and before its first section:

  build/mkdual build/hsdefrag-win.exe dos/cwsdstub.exe build/dos/hsdefrag \
      hsdefrag.exe

The stub's program and relocation table and the COFF image are copied byte
for byte. The MS-DOS header at the start of the file takes the stub's values
(memory, stack, entry point) and gives the stub's size, so the stub finds
its COFF image right after itself; Windows follows the header's pointer to
the PE header instead.
